From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 35/35] cuda/hip: pin the shared-expert gate and fold it into the down projection (+1.6% decode, byte-exact)

After 0034, `k_bin_bcast` is down to 81 launches per decode token, and 40 of
them are one line:

    ffn_shexp = ggml_mul(ffn_shexp[2048], sigmoid(shared_gate)[1])

A 2048-element pass over the shared expert's output to apply ONE scalar,
once per layer: 1.84 us of kernel plus ~1.5 us of graph-replay overhead each.
0034's `dst_scale` epilogue already knows how to apply exactly this - a plain
matvec has one destination channel, so the "per-channel factor" is a single
broadcast scalar - and the shared-expert down projection is an individual mmvq
launch (2048 rows, 40/token, 3.45 us), not a grouped one.

The fold nevertheless did not fire, and the reason is graph ORDER rather than
anything about the kernels. Built in source order the layer emits

    ... ffn_shexp (MUL_MAT) , shared_expert_gate_sigmoid (UNARY) , MUL ...

because the sigmoid is created after the shared-expert FFN and the topological
visit reaches it through the multiply. The pair is not adjacent, so no
two-node subgraph matches - and, worse, the scalar is not even computed yet
when the projection runs, so folding it there would have been a read of stale
memory. Creating the gate before the FFN and pinning it with
`ggml_build_forward_expand` (the 0018/0032 trick - creation order does not set
cgraph order, the pins do) puts the sigmoid ahead of the projection and leaves
`MUL_MAT, MUL` adjacent. Ops, operands and shapes are unchanged; only the
position of one node moves.

So this patch is two halves that only work together:

1. `qwen35moe.cpp`: pin `shared_expert_gate_sigmoid` ahead of the shared-expert
   FFN. `GGML_QWEN_SHEXP_GATE_PIN=0` restores the stock order.
2. `ggml-cuda.cu` / `mmvq.cu`: extend the 0034 detector to plain `MUL_MAT`
   (one token, one channel, a single contiguous f32 factor), placed AFTER the
   grouped-mmvq collector so a groupable matvec still prefers grouping.
   `GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SHEXP_GATE_FUSE=1` disables it.

Bit-exact for the same reason 0034 is: the multiplicand is the same rounded
f32 the stock kernel would have written and the MUL would have read back, and
the light `has_dst_scale` instantiation avoids the `result += bias` that costs
-0.0 (see 0034).

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0), both halves toggled together:

- same-binary toggle A/B, 5 interleaved rounds: tg128 139.40-139.70 off ->
  **141.64-141.94** on, median per-round ratio **1.0161**, 5/5 disjoint;
  pp512 neutral (4285-4324 off vs 4287-4330 on)
- dispatches 901 -> 861 per token; k_bin_bcast 81 -> 41
- whole-process stock vs cand, 5 rounds: see README
- ppl 3.9271/3.9264/3.9277/3.9262 vs stock 3.9314 (-0.09 to -0.13%);
  control same session 3.9271-3.9297
- test-backend-ops MUL_MAT / MUL_MAT_ID / MUL / SSM_CONV / GATED_DELTA_NET OK
- server greedy **6/6 BYTE-EXACT** vs the pin-off/fold-off control

Measurement note burned into this round: a `python3 edit.py && make` that is
piped through `tail` hides both a failed edit and a skipped build, and the
next A/B then measures the PREVIOUS binary. That produced a full round of
"the reorder alone is worth +1.7%, the fold is worth nothing" before the .so
timestamp gave it away. Check the artifact mtime, not the console tail.

---

